e the former  president who was a businessman and of  course zielinski was a was a comedian  before so he's saying you know it's it's  poroshenko that's the idiot that is the  sort of fool now because  he's been doing sort of publicity stunts  with holding a rifle and swearing  legions and lots of stuff whereas the  feeling is that  is having a very very good war um but as  i say you have to not take everything  with a pinch of salt so much but you  have to be aware that these guys are  sort of on a wall footing so they're not  going to say anything that they think a  western journalist will construe as  doubt  or uh fear or was clear they don't mind  missing but doubts also that they don't  want us to convey the wrong message  freddie i know you've also been to the  border where you're witnessing people  trying to pass from ukraine into poland  i imagine those are some very difficult  very upsetting scenes  it was it was a pretty depressing scene  yes there was a there was kind of  crossing over from poland there was this  enormously long line  of migrants  uh north african migrants um who  apparently have come from the east  of ukraine they got there pretty quickly  considering this was only day three or  four of the conflict  and they were standing around they said  they'd been there for days  and it was quite i mean uh funny and  tragic at the same time there was this  chap just came up to me and said um  uh is this moldova and he was pointing  at the um the polish border  uh and i had to tell him uh that no  poland uh but he didn't seem too much to  mind that much but he then just trotted  back to his queue  and they all look very bored and quite  scared  yeah  yeah um freddie last question in your  time in la vie what have you been most  surprised by or what have you been there  surprised to see  um  i suppose i've been surprised by  uh  you know the passion i mean i always you  know  one reads a lot about the passion but  because you know one tends journalist  tends to try and be cynical or whatever  you kind of think i must be hyped but  it's not  they're all very very committed  uh and it's kind of it's inspiring but  it's also pretty frightening because you  realize how serious they are about this  and how serious this war could get uh  very quickly  freddie thanks for taking the time stay  safe  in his column for the magazine this week  douglas murray writes about the  right-wing fringe who are supporting  vladimir putin saying he's an antidote  to the west's woke culture to explain  douglas joins me now  douglas thanks so much for joining  spectator tv this week you write in your  column for the magazine that the fringes  of the american right are getting  vladimir putin all wrong can you explain  yes it's not just the american rights i  say parts of the european right and and  even perhaps you could say parts of the  british right have for years um  taken a sort of contrarian view about  putin you know  for years the cold warriors and their  descendants  thought one thing which was you know  putin was an aggressor he was a  strategic threat  and that russia under him uh was going  to remain some kind of challenge to the  west uh but all the time a new  generation was coming along and i don't  just mean age-wise i mean inevitably a  generation that grew up after the cold  war might be more susceptible to to this  view but but older people as well took a  different view which was among other  things that vladimir putin  presented a sort of um  counterweight or counterbalance to some  of the worst madnesses in our own  society i give examples in the piece uh  not least from uh some american  congresswoman which is perhaps most  alarming um but others as well who said  things like well you know  there was a guest on steve bannon's  podcast recently who said who said well  you know in russia they don't they don't  have any problem knowing which bathroom  to use  that sort of thing that because our own  societies were going mad with the sort  of woke stuff that somehow a society  which didn't have any truck with any of  that or even the most basic rights for  lgbt people for instance uh acted as a  sort of admirable counterweight and in  time this this i think has grown and i  show a little bit of how that has  happened uh in the media in politics and  elsewhere where uh where this this  strange new sort of interpretation of  vladimir putin originated and from there  grew  and douglas to flag up some other  examples in your column this was very  obvious at the far right america first  political action conference last month  where attendees were actually chanting  putin putin putin  how did any part of the american right  get to that state  well this this it should be said is an  incredibly fringe part of the american  right it's led by an exceptionally  unpleasant little anti-semite called  fuentes and he he he's one of a  generation of works described as sort of  people who've grown up online and who  think of themselves or described as sort  of edge lords these are really pathetic  people who who like to sort of  play with the dark stuff you might say  play with the things you're not meant to  play around with there are people on the  left who do the same thing you know who  sort of i've written about this in the  past who sort of um  play around with stalinism and other  things knowing how dangerous it is  knowing how unpleasant and upsetting it  is but it's slightly reveling in it and  there has emerged also of a tiny segment  but  a significant thing to note on the  american right which does the same thing  which is for instance as fuentos himself  is sort of into holocaust minimization  if not full holocaust denial uh  holocaust joking certainly a sort of um  all sorts of spreading of ugly  anti-semitic conspiracy theories and  more and one of their main things  they've treated themselves to in this  sort of quasi-sexual thrill which as i  say in the piece is possibly because  they know no other type of sexual thrill  this type of person sort of treated  themselves to um to admiring putin  knowing that this brings condemnation  down on them to some extent but rather  reveling in it so yes at this ugly ugly  uh far-right gathering which  unbelievably the idiot congresswoman  marjorie taylor-green actually spoke to  spoke at  she said she didn't hear the chance of  putin putin putin which happened just  before she came on and things like this  they sort of burst out with their  enthusiasm for putin i think among other  things these people do it i mean it's  not worth lingering over them because  they're not a significant political  force but i think it's a sort of example  of something people who who think our  societies are so decadent that that we  should admire the strong man in the  kremlin of course there are so many  things that are wrong with this  not the least of which is that they take  vladimir putin at his own estimation of  himself like they take uh there's  another one that goes around a lot  online which is a sort of comparison of  the cia recruitment video which i  actually wrote about recently in the  spectator it was  rather ridiculous intersectionalist  recruitment campaign they compare that  sort of thing and the sort of you know  um diversity higher american armed  forces campaigns and they stick that  alongside the recruitment ads for the  russian military where you see these  sort of tough robot looking you know  macho guys and they  the these people on the right in america  europe and elsewhere have sort of taken  this estimate  of the kremlins about itself for  themselves as well  and as i say in the pieces i mean it's  very interesting because  yes exactly they've basically fallen for  a form of russian propaganda which is we  are tough you are weak we are strong  uh you are  uh you know into all of this sort of  woke nonsense and  as i say it it should be kind of  obvious to any thinking conservative  that that