se  it and trump has has also condemned it  but trump made his reference the fact  it's so clever of him to have sent in  these peacekeeping forces you know  they're the strongest peacekeeping  forces i've ever seen it's it that's  that's a little bit more than uh  an admiration for a tactical uh a  tactical adversary  that's a sort of admiration for this  tactic  and as i say that's that's not that's  not someone on the fringes social media  may have tried to push donald trump to  the fringes but he's not on the fringes  and i think that there is this there is  this sort of  continuous line on the right  where this temptation to see as i say  because of the deracination of our own  institutions  because of some of the idiocies which  we've well rehearsed on this show before  some of the idiocies in our own society  we overestimate and indeed in some  people's cases positively salivate over  the fact that certain other regimes  particularly dictatorship like that of  vladimir putin doesn't have any of these  problems and can treat themselves to  being more serious in the world as i say  i think it's a massive moral error it's  a strategic error and it also simply  falls for the kremlin's own estimation  of itself  douglas thanks for joining me  and finally i'm joined by james forsyth  and katie balls to give us the latest  from westminster  katie james thanks so much for joining  spectator tv james first what do you  make of the government's response to the  russian invasion of ukraine over the  past week  i think a lot of the strategic decisions  that the uk took have been  vindicated by events i think one of the  reasons why  the uh russian advance in ukraine has  slowed is in part because  of the uk's decision to  send lethal aid to the ukrainian forces  before this conflict began it is  remarkable to think that six weeks ago  uh london didn't even dare ask berlin  for fear of refusal whether it could fly  weapons to kiev over german airspace  yet you now see 20 