ps if you were to have  the police announce a fixed penalty  notice for the prime minister in the  coming days i would now be skeptical  that would lead to a confidence vote and  i don't think it means that boris  johnson's position is rosy i mean i  think that's  in a way we're in a very somber time if  you think about the situation in ukraine  russia's invasion and but i think that  uh  for the next you know few months this is  just not something which is at the top  of people's minds and instead there is a  sense that it's a very grave situation  and the best thing that you can have is  unity in the tory party and also i think  a sense of unity across the house you  saw it um when david lammy the shadow  foreign secretary was speaking this week  he said you know the putin must also  deal the united kingdom and i think that  you're seeing fewer partisan attacks  between the sides i think during the  pandemic you often heard um both labour  and the tories saying they weren't going  to have partisan attacks and then they  would actually just do that and whereas  i think in this situation there is a  more realistic prospect of that  james something that may be on voter's  minds however is the growing cost of  living crisis uh which was set to be bad  before the sanctions hit but now with  the sanctions especially on energy could  really see bills going through  the roof how serious is that effect  going to be and has the government done  enough to prepare people  for what they're about to experience  i think it is going to be very serious  and i don't think the government has  done enough you know in his speech in  poland this week boris johnson had a  line talking about how you know dealing  with this crisis was going to and the  sanctions on russia were going to impose  costs at home  as well and that energy bills would go  up but i think he needs to address  people more directly on this point i'm  very struck by the fact that both uh joe  biden and emmanuel macron have used  their addresses to their voters to speak  directly about this point i think boris  johnson needs to get people ready for  the fact that energy prices and food  prices are going to go up because of  this conflict  and katie last question to you if we are  looking at a very serious cost of living  crisis how is boris johnson ever going  to deliver on his leveling up agenda and  the other promises that he made when he  went into number 10 in the medium to  long term it still feels as if the  conservative party's prospects are a lot  worse than they were a few months ago  yeah there's lots of big questions i  think that have been raised by the  escalating situation in ukraine and  we've already seen for example and james  is talking about this this big shift  across europe so um the germans are  increasing defense spending and i i mean  i think it's quite clear there's going  to be a push for the uk to expand to  expand its defense budget going forward  and you also save a situation where cost  of living is going to get worse even  worse i think than the predictions that  we um had  pre-ukraine and the russian invasion and  inflation could even potentially hit  double figures um so you put all these  things together that's not an easy  situation for any you know governing  party to be in and it makes things like  leveling up harder but i also think this  goes back to james's point which is  effectively if you have a situation  where um the prime minister is able to  level the public and actually not really  um you know present this as a choice so  there is no choice here is this or  actually allowing russia to keep going  where will that ever end and you will  bring people inside but it's going to be  tough and if i were the tories you'd  want to almost bring labour in on some  of these points given that is currently  unity in the house of commons broadly  speaking on ukraine and russia  and i think that if boris johnson starts  to talk about rising prices w